Subject: gcc/c-family: default the C dialect to gnu17

GCC 16 defaults C to gnu23, whose "()" == "(void)" prototype rule rejects
the K&R function declarations (`char *getcwd ();` and friends) that are
pervasive in the older autotools/gnulib code making up substrate's contrib
userland (gettext, etc.).  Default the C front end to gnu17 instead; an
explicit -std=... on the command line still overrides.  C++ is untouched.

---
--- a/gcc/c-family/c-opts.cc
+++ b/gcc/c-family/c-opts.cc
@@ -261,8 +261,13 @@
 
   if (c_language == clk_c)
     {
-      /* The default for C is gnu23.  */
-      set_std_c23 (false /* ISO */);
+      /* Substrate: default C to gnu17, not gnu23.  GCC 16's gnu23 default
+	 makes an empty "()" declaration mean "(void)" (zero args), which
+	 rejects the K&R function declarations pervasive in the older
+	 autotools/gnulib code that makes up substrate's userland
+	 (e.g. gettext's `char *getcwd ();`).  An explicit -std=... still
+	 overrides this.  C++ is unaffected (handled separately below).  */
+      set_std_c17 (false /* ISO */);
 
       /* If preprocessing assembly language, accept any of the C-family
 	 front end options since the driver may pass them through.  */
